  "slug": "partition-equal-subset-sum",
  "title": "Partition Equal Subset Sum",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Knapsack",
  "patterns": [
    "knapsack",
    "subset sum",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "if the total sum is odd, is an equal split even possible? what does that tell you about a necessary first check?",
    "if the total is even, the problem becomes: does some subset of nums sum to exactly totalSum / 2? (the rest automatically sums to the other half)",
    "this is 0/1 subset sum - define dp[s] as true if some subset of the numbers processed so far sums to exactly s, with dp[0] = true always",
    "for each number num, update dp[s] = dp[s] OR dp[s - num] for every s from the target down to num - the high-to-low direction matters, same reasoning as the 0/1 knapsack rolling array",
    "after processing every number, the answer is dp[totalSum / 2]"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting the odd-total-sum early exit, and trying to search for a target that can never be reached",
    "iterating the subset-sum update from low to high, which lets a single number be used more than once (turns it into unbounded, giving wrong answers)",
    "using target = totalSum instead of totalSum / 2"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of positive integers `nums`, where every element must belong to exactly one of two subsets.\n\n**Determine whether it's possible to split the list into two subsets such that the sum of elements in each subset is equal.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 200",
      "1 <= nums[i] <= 100",
      "the sum of all elements in nums does not exceed 20000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "total sum is odd (immediately impossible, return false)",
      "a single element array (can never be split into two non-empty... actually only impossible unless trivially handled by sum being 0, which can't happen with positive integers, so always false)",
      "an array where one element equals half the total sum exactly"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
